// Kremling2018 (run_study.sh adds conf/Kremling2018.config to every batch).
// 3' mRNA-seq FWD (poly(T)-primed), NextSeq 500 single-end 85-90 nt, untrimmed, no UMIs.
// Checked 29 Oct 2026 on the first 200,000 reads of 11 runs (pipeline/read_structure/):
//  - salmon finds stranded-forward reads (SF; SR about 2% of SF);
//  - 25-42% of reads run into a poly(A) tail (median start at nt 44-51), followed by a degraded
//    TruSeq adapter and G runs (no signal on two-colour chemistry);
//  - the first ~12 nt carry random-primer composition bias, but removing them lowers mapping
//    by 3 points with salmon, so they stay.
// fastp therefore treats an 18-nt poly(A) as the adapter (cuts the tail and everything after it),
// then trims 3' poly(G) and other homopolymers. Mapping on the test reads: 35-53% untrimmed,
// 37-58% with the Xue2021 settings, 42-68% with these.
// No length correction: a 3' tag counts once per transcript whatever its length.
// From 29 Oct 2026 ~22:00 (LeafMid ran with them): no fq lint and no FastQC. Each Slurm job on
// Hazel runs a task epilog that makes ~28 controller requests as the user, against a per-user
// limit of 6 per second, which capped us at ~12 finished jobs a minute. These steps were 4 of the
// ~6 jobs per library and add nothing needed here: fastp reports read quality, adapters and
// duplication, the FASTQ were checked on download (read counts or MD5), and library QC uses
// salmon's output.
params {
    extra_fastp_args        = '--adapter_sequence AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA --trim_poly_g --trim_poly_x --length_required 20'
    extra_salmon_quant_args = '--noLengthCorrection'
    skip_linting            = true
    skip_fastqc             = true
}
